{"step_index":121,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-28T03:28:35Z","thinking":"The current behavior appends new tasks to the bottom of the task list, not respecting the hierarchical structure. The user wants new tasks like A1.3 to be inserted after their logical predecessor, A1.2. This suggests a need to modify the task insertion logic to maintain the Work Breakdown Structure (WBS) ordering.\n\n\nInitial hypothesis: Implement WBS sorting using a hierarchical code comparator. The comparator should parse task codes into segments (e.g., \"A1.2\" becomes ['A', 1, 2]). Compare codes segment by segment to determine the correct insertion position, considering both major phase ordering and sub-task numerical sequencing. This requires an understanding of how the user defines the order.\n\n\nThe hierarchical code comparator needs to consider segment-by-segment comparison, like 'A1.2' before 'A1.3'. Major sections ('A', 'L', 'B', 'C') require specific ordering logic, possibly determined by user-defined project structure or a default order. The insertion logic must account for both numerical sub-task hierarchy within each section and the defined overall section sequence. Need to ensure preservation of existing major section order (or a custom order if provided). The insertion point needs to be calculated based on existing tasks and the new task code's position in the WBS.\n\n\nConsidering two insertion strategies: automatic insertion after a predecessor or a full WBS sort. Adding a user interface option for either \"automatic WBS\" insertion or specifying a predecessor for insertion enhances user experience and control. The current hypothesis focuses on a smart WBS sorting system that handles code parsing into segments, allowing for hierarchical comparisons, while preserving the user's defined order. This smart system should support both new additions and full re-sorts.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"& \"C:\\Program Files\\nodejs\\node.exe\" -e \"\nconst majorOrder = ['A', 'L', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E', 'F'];\n\nfunction parseWbs(code) {\n  if (!code) return { majorIdx: 999, segments: [] };\n  const match = code.match(/^([A-Za-z]+)(.*)$/);\n  if (!match) return { majorIdx: 999, segments: [code] };\n  const letter = match[1].toUpperCase();\n  const rest = match[2];\n  let majorIdx = majorOrder.indexOf(letter);\n  if (majorIdx === -1) majorIdx = 100 + letter.charCodeAt(0);\n  \n  // rest might be '1.2' or '1' or ''\n  const segments = rest ? rest.split('.').map(s => {\n    const num = parseInt(s, 10);\n    return isNaN(num) ? s : num;\n  }) : [];\n  return { majorIdx, segments };\n}\n\nfunction compareWbs(a, b) {\n  const pa = parseWbs(a);\n  const pb = parseWbs(b);\n  if (pa.majorIdx !== pb.majorIdx) return pa.majorIdx - pb.majorIdx;\n  const len = Math.max(pa.segments.length, pb.segments.length);\n  for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) {\n    const sa = pa.segments[i];\n    const sb = pb.segments[i];\n    if (sa === undefined) return -1; // shorter (parent) comes first, e.g. A1 before A1.1\n    if (sb === undefined) return 1;\n    if (typeof sa === 'number' && typeof sb === 'number') {\n      if (sa !== sb) return sa - sb;\n    } else {\n      const cmp = String(sa).localeCompare(String(sb));\n      if (cmp !== 0) return cmp;\n    }\n  }\n  return 0;\n}\n\nconst list = ['C1', 'A2', 'A1.2', 'A1', 'A', 'A1.3', 'A1.1', 'L1', 'B1', 'A2.1'];\nlist.sort(compareWbs);\nconsole.log(list.join(' -> '));\n\"","Cwd":"C:\\Users\\GiaWyy\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\aps-project-manager","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Testing WBS sorting algorithm","toolSummary":"Test WBS sorter in Node"}}]}
